Vitamin D functions as a hormone that regulates calcium metabolism and helps maintain blood calcium levels by increasing absorption from the gut and kidneys (https://www.hra.nhs.uk/planning-and-improving-research/application-summaries/research-summaries/vitamin-d-chocolate). It is found in oily fish, egg yolks, red meat, liver, some mushrooms and fortified foods such as breakfast cereals and fat spreads (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). Public Health England advises a daily supplement of 10 micrograms during autumn and winter (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d)

