Vitamin D is a fat‑soluble vitamin that functions as a hormone to regulate calcium and phosphate, which are needed for bone, teeth and muscle health (Sandwell and West Birmingham CCG formulary, 2016).  
It is found in foods such as oily fish, egg yolk, red meat, liver, certain mushrooms and fortified products like breakfast cereals and spreads (Barnsley CCG guidance, 2024; NHS Live Well, 2024).  
Public Health England advises that adults consider taking a supplement of 10 micrograms (µg) or 400 IU per day during the autumn and winter months (NHS, 2023).  

