Iron is needed to make haemoglobin, or ‘Hb’, which carries oxygen around the body and gives blood its red colour [https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet]. It is also important for building and maintaining healthy muscles and for many of the body’s functions and reactions [https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet]. Animal‑based sources such as liver, kidney, red meat and sardines, and plant‑based sources including pulses, dark‑green leafy vegetables, tofu, nuts, seeds and fortified cereals are rich in iron [https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet]. The UK reference intake is 8.7 mg per day for adult men and women over 50; 14.8 mg per day for women aged 19‑50 [https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/iron-bda.pdf].

