Folate (vitamin B9) is a B‑complex vitamin that helps the body make DNA and RNA, which are needed for cell formation, and it works with vitamin B12 to produce red blood cells and support nervous system health (Whittington NHS Trust leaflet, https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=374). It is found in fortified cereals and bread as well as natural sources such as oranges, green vegetables, peas, liver and soybeans (Whittington NHS Trust leaflet, https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=374). Good dietary sources also include spinach, kale, Brussels sprouts, broccoli, beans, yeast, beef extracts, oranges, wheat bran, poultry, pork, shellfish and fortified foods such as breakfast cereals (Sussex NHS PDF, https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf). The typical adult reference intake is 0.2 mg per day, and for those aged 11 years and over it is 200 μg per day (Hey NHS leaflet, https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/folate-information-for-renal-patients-information-to-increase-folate-in-your-diet; Sussex NHS PDF, https://www.uhsussex.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/folicacidbda.pdf).

