Zinc is required for making new cells and enzymes, processing carbohydrates, fats and proteins, supporting wound healing, maintaining immune function, and helping the body stay balanced, digest food, grow and develop, and keep skin and hair healthy (NHS – Others – vitamins and minerals; Whittington NHS leaflet). It is found in foods such as meat, shellfish, dairy products, bread, fortified breakfast cereals, legumes, nuts, seeds, salmon and cod (NHS – Others – vitamins and minerals; Whittington NHS leaflet; NHS Lanarkshire food sources leaflet). The typical adult reference intake in the UK is about 9.5 mg per day (NHS – Others – vitamins and minerals), and the upper intake level for supplemental zinc is 25 mg per day (NHS – Others – vitamins and minerals).

